;; db: bench_stardriver
;; DATA-BLIND DRIVER, 3-join variant (semijoin threshold IS met, yet does
;; not rescue).  Path X -> hub -> Z -> W filtered by a tiny selective `sa`:
;;
;;   sa(X,_)  3 rows selective     a(X,H) N rows H:=0     (fan-in to hub)
;;   b(H,Z)   N rows H=0           c(Z,W) N rows identity (a|><|b = N^2)
;;
;; sel-driven: O(N).  big-driven: O(N^2).  With 3 joins the semijoin filter
;; DOES fire, but it only trims ~15% -- filtering cannot undo driving from
;; the large relation, you still enumerate the huge driver x join.  So the
;; ~100x source-order gap survives the full default machinery.  Proof that
;; the data-blind driver problem is NOT confined to sub-threshold queries.
table (sa int int)
table (a int int)
table (b int int)
table (c int int)
table (ans int int)

rule (a X H) (b H Z) (c Z W) (sa X D) --> (ans X W)
